sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (proposed 2026-11-06): build the nested fleet of a task VM from stock Ubuntu with Orbit's own steps, when a host has no ready copies (new host, new image, or broken copies). The operator prepares the Gateway once with a local Swamp run (Swamp binary, Ansible venv, swamp/ folder, key); the Gateway then converges itself and runs orbit:bootstrap; app-dev and app-prod join through the nested Gateway's own node:add. Ends where stage 6 "Save the new images" starts.
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: none · nested fleet build from scratch
  %% kind: task_vm.fleet_build
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane VM icon=server pill=operator tone=purple
  %% lane NG icon=gateway pill=nested tone=amber
  %% lane NN icon=server pill=nodes tone=gray
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant VM as Refresh VM<br/>outer · operator
  participant NG as Nested Gateway<br/>with Swamp
  participant NN as Nested Nodes<br/>app-dev · app-prod
  Note over GW: Admit the fleet build<br/>host · release commit
  %%> When:
  %%> - a task VM host whose seed has no usable ready copies; part of stage 6 for a new host or a new image
  %%> Result:
  %%> - full intent recorded: the refresh VM, the nested subnet, the three Node names and addresses, the release commit
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-fleet-build
  SW->>VM: Create the nested network<br/>subnet · three stock VMs
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - in the refresh VM: the nested Incus network from the intent's subnet, then three VMs from the stock Ubuntu image, each with cloud-init for the `orbit` account, the key that reaches it and the NFS client (`nfs-common`) that mounts the checkout, a 20 GiB root disk and a state volume as large as its memory (the copies are stateful), all on the pool `topology-seed` of the seed disk, so the ready copies are copies in the seed
  %%> - every action one `@swamp/ssh exec` argv on the refresh VM
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - a rebuild in the same VM removes the earlier fleet and forgets the earlier Gateway's host key in the operator's `known_hosts`: the new Gateway has the same address and a new key, and the bootstrap's `@swamp/cfgmgmt` steps trust `known_hosts` on first use (they take no pinned key), so the old entry refused them
  %%> - the refresh VM makes the SSH key of its `orbit` account when it has none (`ssh-keygen`, no passphrase): a fresh task VM has no key, and the key reaches the nested VMs; its private half stays on the refresh VM, which is never published, and the guests keep only its public half
  %%> - the three VMs are created one at a time: three parallel `incus init` calls of the same remote image race on the client's image cache (`mkdir ~/.cache/incus/<fingerprint>: file exists`), which a live run on beast showed
  %%> - the checkout the build reads (the release commit, its remote, the Swamp repository of the bootstrap run) is the checkout of the refresh VM's own workspace, which the intent names (`operator_checkout`): a task VM keeps it under `/home/orbit/apps/<project>/workspace`, not at a fixed path, which a live run on beast showed (`fatal: cannot change to '/home/orbit/orbit'`)
  %%> - the build needs a large task VM (12 GiB, 6 CPUs): the three nested VMs hold 10 GiB (Gateway 4, app-dev 3, app-prod 3), and a medium VM (8 GiB) lost its nested Gateway to the out-of-memory killer during a fleet build (VM 707, and runs 11720 and 11749); the Gateway admits the build only for a large VM, and the admission of the refresh sets the size large, so the Project size lists do not decide it
  %%> - the nested guests mount the export of that same path (`What=` in their mount units) at `/home/orbit/orbit` (`Where=`); the converge of the task VM role exports the workspace checkout, so the units name the export path and not the mount point
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - the ready copies keep the unit of the refresh VM's workspace path, so they serve the task VMs of the Project whose workspace path that is (the proof Project); a Project with another slug needs its own copies or a stable export path, which is a separate change of the task VM role
  VM->>NG: Prepare the Gateway<br/>local Swamp · one run
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the operator (here the refresh VM) runs the Gateway bootstrap workflow once with a local `swamp workflow run`; its steps request no worker, so the binary runs them itself
  %%> - over SSH it installs on the fresh machine the pinned Swamp binary, the swamp role's pinned Ansible venv, the release's `swamp/` folder and the Swamp key
  %%> - the same bootstrap a fresh Orbit install uses: the user's CLI keeps the Swamp binary in `~/.orbit` and removes it after the handover; no Ansible on the user's machine
  %%> - the release is a git checkout at the release commit: its `swamp/` folder is the Gateway's Swamp repository and holds the Ansible pins, and `apps/gateway` is its code
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - the Swamp key is not in this run: the shape gate allows a vault read for a file only in a step placed on 209, and a local run has no placement; the key reaches the machine with `incus file push`, read from a vault copy of the kind (the build is a placed run, so the vault read is allowed), so the local run holds no secret
  %%> - the local run itself needs the key on the operator VM, and only a proof VM gets one at provision; so the build places it there from the same vault copy, on stdin, and removes it after the run, also after a failure, because a task's agent may run on that VM (decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review, 2026-11-06)
  %%> - the Gateway cannot plan any role before its own app runs, and the app needs PHP, Composer and a database; the `gateway` role installs no PHP, so "converge itself, then `orbit:bootstrap`" cannot be the order
  %%> - so the same local run also applies the Ansible `prerequisites` phase of the role playbook with Ansible's local connection; the operator renders its intent from the recipe alone with `php artisan` (no database), so the machine gets its pinned PHP, Caddy and WireGuard packages
  NG->>NG: Converge itself<br/>gateway · websocket · swamp
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - on the machine, in this order: Composer install, the Gateway `.env` (it names its database, which the ready copies and stage 2 read) and application key, migrations, then `orbit:bootstrap`: its CA, SSH key and WireGuard hub; the Gateway records itself and the operator
  %%> - then the Gateway's own `node:role:add --converge` for gateway, websocket and swamp: its own API admits them and its own Swamp runs the normal role phases with Ansible's local connection, starting its Swamp serve and worker
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), built and proven live in #2798, #2813, #2792, #2819, #2822 and #2828:
  %%> - `orbit:bootstrap` is the Gateway's own code and runs no agent command: the Node and role records, the VPN settings, the root CA, the SSH key and the host key of the machine; it writes nothing under `/etc` and starts no service
  %%> - the WireGuard hub waits for Swamp, because its private key is made on the Node; the VPN role stays `provisioning` until then
  %%> - the Swamp Node is the Node that holds the `swamp` role, found by role and not by an id (`SwampNode`), so the nested Gateway needs no special id; recording the host key raises no model event, because it would start a Swamp run before Swamp exists
  %%> - the nested bridge comes from the task VM role phases: Incus is initialised with a pool and the bridge, and the VM firewall opens DHCP, DNS and routing for it
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the Swamp control plane of a fresh Gateway:
  %%> - the same local run applies the `swamp` role's own Ansible phase with a local connection, as it did `prerequisites`: it renders the serve and worker units for the machine's checkout path, mints the serve, worker and driver tokens on the machine with the Swamp CLI, creates the control vault and its key there, writes the Gateway's grants and starts the serve and worker
  %%> - the tokens never leave the machine, as for any fresh Orbit install that owns its own Swamp credentials
  %%> - after that, `node:role:add` goes through the machine's own Swamp for everything else: gateway, websocket and the `swamp.orbit` route
  %%> - the swamp role's own runtime steps are native workflow steps bound to worker labels, not an Ansible phase, and a local run refuses a step that asks for a worker; so the bootstrap has its own Ansible `swamp` phase (`gateway-bootstrap.yml --tags swamp`) with the same units and the same tokens, and a Caddy front for `swamp.orbit` on port 443 signed by the Gateway's CA, because the Gateway starts runs over TLS to that name
  %%> - from here on its own Swamp does everything
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the first role run of a fresh Gateway:
  %%> - the control plane phase issues the Gateway's own certificate for `gateway.orbit` from the machine's CA, as for `swamp.orbit`, and installs it with the root certificate at the paths the rendered Caddyfile names (`/etc/caddy/orbit-cert-current`); the first role run publishes that Caddyfile and needs them
  %%> - the control plane phase starts private DNS with empty records and an empty catalog and restarts dnsmasq on its loopback listener, so the first publication of a private name, in the first role run, finds both
  %%> - `orbit:gateway-web` is retired with its action: it only refused, and the role now does what it did
  %%> - the `swamp.orbit` Route is recorded in the same bootstrap, before the first Caddyfile publish, so a publication never drops the front the Gateway reaches Swamp through
  %%> - the rest of the Gateway role comes from the `gateway` role converge itself: the PHP-FPM pool and the Caddy site (its API), and units for the scheduler and Horizon, which record finished runs; a Gateway whose Node has a `schedule-work` or `horizon` Process keeps those, and Horizon runs only where a queue is configured
  %%> - the queue is a Valkey server on the machine and its Redis Database connection `gateway-queue`, recorded by the bootstrap with the other records, because Operations need a queue to record a finished run
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the operator record:
  %%> - `orbit:bootstrap --operator-ip` records the Node `operator` (no roles, the task VM's address 10.45.0.10, no key) with access to the Gateway Node, so it holds Gateway authority; the build never added it, so stage 2 recorded the operator key on no row and the nested Gateway refused the operator (`peer.identity_unknown`)
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), a Gateway that records its first run:
  %%> - the Gateway records a finished run through its scheduler and Horizon, and the `gateway` role brings their units; the first run, the converge of its own Node, comes before that role, so nothing recorded it (op 11625 waited out its budget on a run that had succeeded)
  %%> - so the control plane phase also writes and starts the same two units (`GatewayRuntimeFiles::recorder`), after serve and the workers; the role later finds them unchanged
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the Gateway's own Node before the others join:
  %%> - the Gateway Node is a Node like the others: the control plane phase makes its agent key and records only the verifier, and the first step of the nested Gateway is `node:converge` of its own Node, which installs its agent and CLI and publishes private DNS
  %%> - the `vpn` role is active when the hub runs, and the `swamp` role is active when serve and both workers run; neither needs a run of its own
  %%> - the machine trusts the Orbit root certificate, and the `caddy` user joins the account's group, because services on it call `gateway.orbit` over TLS and Caddy reads the checkout
  %%> - the `websocket` role is added to the Gateway Node before app-dev and app-prod: the agents report through it
  SW->>NG: Add app-dev and app-prod<br/>node:add · nested Orbit
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the nested Gateway's own `node:add` with the roles `app-dev` and `app-prod`; its own Swamp converges them over SSH
  %%> - so a fleet build also proves node:add and the role phases on fresh machines
  %%> - the Nodes are named after their roles, `app-dev` and `app-prod` (the guests stay `nested-app-dev` and `nested-app-prod`): the proof runner and the cases find a Node by its role name
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the package cache:
  %%> - the Composer tool setup of a nested Node refreshes the apt indexes of the Ubuntu snapshot; for a Node inside the nested subnet its plan reads them from the `apt-cacher-ng` of the outer VM (`http://10.232.2.1:3142`, apt proxy) instead of `https://snapshot.ubuntu.com`; the packages stay a direct download
  %%> - the outer VM role installs and starts the cache and opens port 3142 on the nested bridge only; the image build fills it (`vm-image-build.mmd`), so a fleet build finds the indexes warm
  %%> - the Gateway names the cache for a Node whose address is in the nested subnet `10.232.2.0/24`; the e2e harness never hands out that subnet, so no other Node is inside it. Any other Node reads the snapshot directly
  %%> - the Node asks for the same index files as the image build (no `apt.conf.d` drop-ins) and one request at a time (no pipelining, as in the image build)
  NG->>NN: Enrol and converge<br/>WireGuard · roles · agent
  SW->>NN: Open the proof network<br/>ufw allow nested subnet
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - on each of the three guests, one `ufw allow from 10.232.2.0/24 comment proof-network`, the subnet from the Gateway's intent
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the proof starts from a fleet where only Orbit's own rules and this allowance exist (`swamp/proof/batches.yaml`, `fresh-fleet`); the cases probe over the nested subnet and place their test rules above this row, so a copy without it leaves every case `unrun`
  SW->>NG: Mount the checkout<br/>NFS from the outer VM
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the Gateway and app-dev guests stop the Gateway services, move `.env`, `storage` and `bootstrap/cache` (and the Swamp repository's own state) to `/var/lib/orbit-checkout-state` on their own disk, and drop the checkout they built
  %%> - the stock systemd units of stage 2 then mount the outer VM's export at `/home/orbit/orbit` and the state over it, and the services start again, now on the shared checkout
  %%> - the export, its firewall rule and the nested network come from the task VM role phases of the outer VM (`task-vms:converge`); the build adds no mechanism
  %%> Why:
  %%> - the ready copies keep no checkout: every task VM mounts its own over NFS, so a fleet that is saved must already run that way
  SW->>NG: Check readiness<br/>agents · Swamp · Doctor
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the same check as stage 2: every agent online, the Swamp worker enrolled, Doctor has no finding
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the kind `task_vm.fleet_build`:
  %%> - the kind targets a ready task VM, as `task_vm.ready_copies` does, and ends at the Doctor check of the nested Gateway; the save of the ready copies follows as its own kind
  %%> - the artisan commands of the nested Gateway run with `--env HOME=/home/orbit`, because `incus exec --user` leaves `HOME` unset and the commands would look for the state of the Gateway under `/root`
  %%> - the nested VMs have fixed addresses and MACs in the Gateway's intent, because the ready copies store them and the records of the nested Gateway name them
  %%> - each Operation of the nested Gateway is admitted with an artisan command (`orbit:role-add`, `orbit:node-converge`, `orbit:node-add`); the nested Gateway has no CLI to follow it
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06):
  %%> - a wait for an Operation of the nested Gateway, and the wait for its Doctor, is one Ansible task run from Node 209 against the refresh VM (`task-vm-fleet-wait.yml`): `ansible.builtin.command` with the tool's own `until`, `retries` and `delay`, one `incus exec` per check, every 5 seconds, bounded by the seconds the step gives it (each check takes some seconds on top of the delay, so the step's literal `timeout` is twice that and only guards against a hung connection); no shell loop
  %%> - a wait stops as soon as the Gateway guest answers `Instance is not running`, and when it ends the task lists the nested VMs and fails naming any that is not running; so a dead nested VM fails the wait within seconds and not at its timeout (runs 11720 and 11749 lost the nested Gateway during the enrollment of app-dev, and the shell loop ran to its 2,000 second timeout)
  %%> - an Operation that ends without `succeeded` is read again for its `failed_step` and `error_code`, and the failure names them (op 11821: `app-dev was not added` hid why the nested enrollment failed after 14 minutes)
  %%> - the task also asserts the end state (the Operation `succeeded`, Doctor healthy), so the separate `Check` steps are gone; the last wait is the step `Check the nested fleet`, which the Gateway reads as the end of the run
  %%> - the guests take their checkout from the outer VM's NFS export, as in stage 2, before the copies are saved: the build uses the same stock mount units as the copies, so the save finds them in place
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the lifetimes in a fleet that is saved:
  %%> - the nested Gateway makes its own root CA at bootstrap with a 10-year life and signs its leaves for 397 days; the fleet is private to a disposable task VM, so a long CA costs nothing, and a seed never holds a CA that runs out in days
  %%> - the Swamp key is written to `api-key`, the file the nested Swamp units read and a task VM pushes; the ready copies delete that file from the running Gateway and scan for the key, so a copy holds none
  %%> - the nested Swamp tokens last 30 days, longer than a seed lives, because the daily refresh replaces the seed; the 24-hour tokens and 2-day certificates of the earlier hand-made fleet are not made again
  alt the fleet is ready
    SW->>VM: Save the ready copies<br/>stage 6 · task_vm.ready_copies
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the existing step: key-free, self-contained copies with the network stored, operator key removed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>seed has ready copies
  else a step failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the refresh VM and its nested VMs are destroyed; the host keeps the seed it had
  end
